  "title": "IBM 6:5 Cartridge System (1975)",
  "year": "1975",
  "subtitle": "The office dictation machine that stored your voice on magnetic discs you could color-code, mark urgent, and mail — then replayed them under a transcriptionist's foot",
  "why_scout_cares": "The 6:5 was IBM's answer to the cassette-tape office: dictation stored on six-minute magnetic DISCS packed into a floppy-like cartridge, five colors of plastic that turned a voice recording into a tangible, sortable, mailable object. It is a physical-token interface in the truest sense — the data is a disc you hold, color-code, flag as urgent, and put in an envelope — paired with one of computing's most embodied work rituals: a transcriptionist types with both hands while a FOOT pedal drives the playback and ears hear through a headset. The museum has Cauzin Softstrip, iButton, and QuickFare as physical-token artifacts, but nothing that treats the human VOICE as a tangible token, and nothing that splits input across hands, feet, and ears in one workflow.",
  "search_terms": ["IBM 6:5 cartridge system", "IBM dictation machine magnetic disc", "Executary 6:5 recorder", "IBM 6-5 portable recorder", "foot pedal transcription machine"],
