    "title": "IBM 6:5 Cartridge System",
    "year": "1975",
    "by": "IBM Office Products Division (Lexington / Austin / Berlin)",
    "tags": ["Office", "Physical Token", "Input", "Audio", "Commercial"],
    "overview": "The IBM 6:5 Cartridge System was announced in March 1975 by IBM's Office Products Division as a dictation and transcription system built around a genuinely unusual storage medium. Instead of the endless loops of magnetic tape used by competitors, the 6:5 recorded each piece of dictation onto a six-minute magnetic disc, and those discs were packed into a cartridge that looked strikingly like a 3.5-inch floppy disk. The name '6:5' encodes the economics: each disc holds six minutes of speech, and a recorder fitted with two cartridge readers could carry five hours of dictation.\n\nThe 6:5 was sold as a family of machines — the Recorder Type 281, Transcriber Type 282 (which shipped with a foot pedal and headset), and the Portable Recorder Type 284 — plus a range of controller units (Micro Control, Tone Control, Dial Control) that let remote users telephone in and dictate over the phone. The American Nurses Association was a documented customer, with staff able to dial in externally and record dictation. IBM described the 6:5 launch as the most extensive advertising campaign in Office Products Division history. The range was withdrawn from marketing on December 31, 1981.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "The voice is a disc",
        "text": "The heart of the 6:5 is its medium. Each six-minute magnetic disc (IBM insisted on spelling it 'disc') was about 3 1/4 inches across, and twenty-five of them lived inside a cartridge the size and shape of a floppy disk. Dictation was segmented disc by disc, and the cartridge came in five colors so that different jobs, people, or priorities could be physically distinguished at a glance. Because the recorded voice lived on a discrete, removable object, a disc or cartridge could be marked as urgent and physically routed for rush transcription — data you could sort, stack, flag, and mail."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The whole-body transcription ritual",
        "text": "Transcription on the 6:5 was a full-body workflow that has all but vanished. The transcriptionist sat with both hands on a keyboard, a headset over the ears, and a foot pedal on the floor. Playback was driven entirely by the foot — press to play, ease off to pause, heel for rewind — so that the hands never left the keys. The interface was split across three body channels at once: fingers for output, foot for transport control, ears for input. It is the canonical bimanual-plus-foot office interface, and the 6:5 was one of its most polished commercial embodiments."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A physical-token sibling to the floppy",
        "text": "The 6:5 cartridge is an almost perfect material echo of the floppy disk, but for VOICE rather than data. IBM designed it (with Eliot Noyes & Associates, who had styled the earlier Executary line) to be handled, color-coded, stacked, and mailed — the same physical-token instinct the museum celebrates in Cauzin's Softstrip (printed barcodes), the Dallas iButton (touch-to-identify chip), and Ascom's QuickFare (notched ticket). It is a reminder that in the 1970s the human voice, like data, had to be poured into an object to travel."
      }
    ],
